From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:45:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] luks2 tests: the serial console on a file, not a socket

Since b190b70845, Haiku's x86 kernel gives up on the serial port for the
rest of the boot the first time put_char() polls the UART 256K times
without being able to send. A QEMU socket chardev holds only about 280
one-byte writes, so when a console script read the socket about 50 ms
late during the kernel's device scan, the kernel turned its serial
output off: the guest finished its test and powered off, and the test
saw no result (2 to 3 % of the boots).

QEMU now writes the serial output to a file and reads the keystrokes
from a FIFO (-chardev file,...,input-path=), which never makes the UART
wait. The run scripts hold the FIFO open, so that QEMU's open of it
doesn't wait for a writer; boot_console.py and loader_console.py follow
the file until QEMU's process has ended. run-ui-test.sh no longer stops
(set -e) before its summary when the installed boot printed no LUKSTEST
lines.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
